## What happens when a user visits www.foobar.com

1. The user types `www.foobar.com` in the browser.
2. The browser asks a DNS resolver to translate the name into an IP address.
   The DNS answers with the `www` record of `foobar.com`, which points to
   `8.8.8.8`.
3. The browser sends an HTTP (or HTTPS) request over TCP/IP to `8.8.8.8`.
4. The request reaches the server, where Nginx (the web server) receives it.
5. If the request is for a static file (image, CSS, JS), Nginx serves it
   directly. If it is for dynamic content, Nginx forwards the request to the
   application server.
6. The application server runs the code from the application files and, if it
   needs data, sends queries to the MySQL database.
7. The database returns the data, the application server builds the page and
   gives it back to Nginx.
8. Nginx sends the HTTP response back to the user's browser, which displays the
   website.

## Specifics

### What is a server
A server is a computer (physical or virtual) that provides resources, data or
services to other computers (clients) over a network. Here, one server hosts
everything needed to run the website: web server, application server, code
base and database.

### What is the role of the domain name
The domain name (`foobar.com`) is a human-readable address for the website.
People remember names more easily than IP addresses. The domain name is
registered with a registrar and, through the DNS, it is translated into the IP
address of the server (`8.8.8.8`).

### What type of DNS record is `www` in `www.foobar.com`
`www` is an **A record** (Address record). It maps the hostname `www` of the
domain `foobar.com` directly to an IPv4 address, here `8.8.8.8`. (A CNAME
record would instead map `www` to another domain name.)

### What is the role of the web server
The web server (Nginx) receives the HTTP/HTTPS requests from users. It serves
static content directly and acts as a reverse proxy, forwarding dynamic
requests to the application server, then returning the response to the user.

### What is the role of the application server
The application server executes the application code (the business logic). It
processes dynamic requests, talks to the database, and generates the dynamic
content (HTML pages, JSON, ...) that the web server sends back to the user.

### What is the role of the database
The database (MySQL) stores, organizes and retrieves the persistent data of the
website (users, articles, orders, ...). The application server reads and writes
data in it using SQL queries.

### What is the server using to communicate with the computer of the user
The server communicates with the user's computer over the network (Internet)
using the **TCP/IP** protocol suite, with **HTTP** (or **HTTPS**, which is HTTP
secured with TLS) as the application protocol.

## Issues with this infrastructure

### SPOF (Single Point Of Failure)
There is only one server, so if it fails (hardware problem, crash, power or
network outage), or if any one component on it fails (Nginx, application
server or MySQL), the whole website goes down.

### Downtime when maintenance is needed
When maintenance is required (for example deploying new code, which requires
restarting the web or application server, or updating the system), the
website is unavailable during that time, because there is no other server to
take over.

### Cannot scale if there is too much incoming traffic
All the components share the resources (CPU, RAM, disk, network) of a single
server. If the traffic grows too much, the server becomes overloaded and
slow, and there is no way to spread the load across several servers. The only
option is to upgrade the machine (vertical scaling), which has limits.
